ght improves emotion performance but reduces text performance. (Bottom) ALPO routes separate format, textual, and acoustic advantages to format, text, and speech tokens. Evaluation Protocol We use four 0–100 automatic metrics. GLM-5 (GLM-5-Team et al. 2026 ) scores response text for Intent Fulfillment (IF), which measures whether the response addresses the user’s intent, while a", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1785916818087", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "chatcmpl-tool-ab554cf9695bc6a9", "name": "Bash", "input": {"command": "python3 -c \"\nimport re\nwith open('/tmp/paper_html.html','r',encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    html=f.read()\nhtml=re.sub(r'<script.*?</script>','',html,flags=re.DOTALL)\nhtml=re.sub(r'<style.*?</style>','',html,flags=re.DOTALL)\ntext=re.sub(r'<[^>]+>',' ',html)\ntext=re.sub(r'&amp;','&',text); text=re.sub(r'&lt;','<',text); text=re.sub(r'&gt;','>',text); text=re.sub(r'&nbsp;',' ',text)\ntext=re.sub(r'\\s+',' ',text)\nprint(text[14000:24000])\n\" 2>&1", "description": "Extract HTML text part 3"}}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "d96a0e59-f473-421d-becb-897d79b2b1c2", "uuid": "4cdcb544-857e-47ca-a6e8-fab82f541528"}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ab554cf9695bc6a9", "type": "tool_result", "content": "held-out DeepSeek-V4-Pro (DeepSeek-AI et al. 2026 ) evaluator scores Response Quality (RQ), covering content, linguistic tone appropriateness, and naturalness. DeepSeek-V4-Pro is not involved in data construction, reward computation, or training. MiDashengLM (Dinkel et al. 2025 ) label agreement yields speech Emotion Accuracy (EA). Emotion Similarity (ES) uses pretrained wav2vec2 (Baevski et al. 2020 ) emotion attributes to combine similarity to paired reference audio with consistency against target-emotion statistics. ES remains optimization-aligned with the acoustic reward, whereas the other metrics provide held-out or complementary evaluation. We additionally validate ES against emotion-specific human judgments. ES correlates with human emotion ratings (Spearman ρ = 0.681 \\rho=0.681 ) and is more sensitive to emotion mismatch than to controlled speaker variation. We use speaker-matched references whenever possible; full results are provided in the appendix. For subjective evaluation, Gemini-3.1-Pro (Google-DeepMind 2026 ) performs pairwise comparisons of textual quality, emotional expression, and overall preference on spoken responses; human listeners provide holistic ratings. Method Preliminary Following the task definition above, the model takes a user speech input x x , infers the underlying intent i i , and generates a spoken response y y . We represent y y as a single interleaved sequence, where y txt y^{\\text{txt}} and y sp y^{\\text{sp}} denote the text-token and speech-token subsequences, respectively. y txt y^{\\text{txt}} primarily carries textual content and intent information, while y sp y^{\\text{sp}} governs emotional and prosodic realization. The training objective is therefore to jointly improve textual response quality with respect to user intent and emotional expression in speech with respect to the target emotion. SFT minimizes the negative log-likelihood of the full reference response under teacher forcing: ℒ SFT ​ ( θ ) = − 𝔼 ( x , y ¯ ) ∼ 𝒟 sft ​ ∑ t = 1 | y ¯ | log ⁡ π θ ​ ( y ¯ t ∣ x , y ¯ < t ) , \\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{SFT}}(\\theta)=-\\mathbb{E}_{(x,\\bar{y})\\sim\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{sft}}}\\sum_{t=1}^{|\\bar{y}|}\\log\\pi_{\\theta}\\!\\left(\\bar{y}_{t}\\mid x,\\bar{y}_{<t}\\right), (1) where y ¯ \\bar{y} is the full interleaved reference response including both text and speech tokens. SFT provides a strong initialization but does not directly target end-task objectives such as intent fulfillment and emotional expressiveness. GRPO (Shao et al. 2024 ) samples G G candidate responses { y g } g = 1 G \\{y_{g}\\}_{g=1}^{G} from the old policy π θ old \\pi_{\\theta_{\\mathrm{old}}} , computes a sequence-level reward R g R_{g} for each, and estimates a group-normalized advantage A g = R g − μ σ A_{g}=\\frac{R_{g}-\\mu}{\\sigma} . The training objective is: ℒ GRPO ( θ ) = − 𝔼 1 G ∑ g = 1 G 1 | y g | ∑ t = 1 | y g | min ( r g , t ( θ ) A g , \\displaystyle\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{GRPO}}(\\theta)=-\\mathbb{E}\\,\\frac{1}{G}\\sum_{g=1}^{G}\\frac{1}{|y_{g}|}\\sum_{t=1}^{|y_{g}|}\\min(r_{g,t}(\\theta)\\,A_{g}, (2) clip ( r g , t ( θ ) , 1 − ϵ , 1 + ϵ ) A g ) + β 𝔼 [ KL ( π θ ∥ π ref ) ] , \\displaystyle\\mathrm{clip}\\!\\left(r_{g,t}(\\theta),\\,1-\\epsilon,\\,1+\\epsilon\\right)A_{g})+\\beta\\,\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[\\mathrm{KL}\\!\\left(\\pi_{\\theta}\\,\\|\\,\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\right)\\right], where r g , t ​ ( θ ) = π θ ​ ( y g , t ∣ x , y g , < t ) π θ old ​ ( y g , t ∣ x , y g , < t ) r_{g,t}(\\theta)=\\frac{\\pi_{\\theta}\\!\\left(y_{g,t}\\mid x,y_{g,<t}\\right)}{\\pi_{\\theta_{\\mathrm{old}}}\\!\\left(y_{g,t}\\mid x,y_{g,<t}\\right)} is the token-level probability ratio. We set β = 0 \\beta=0 and omit the KL penalty, following recent practice (Yu et al. 2025 ) . Limitations of Standard Training Methods SFT. SFT provides a reliable initialization but uniformly optimizes text and speech tokens despite their distinct roles. For the same response text y txt y^{\\text{txt}} and target emotion e ⋆ e^{\\star} , multiple speech realizations may be appropriate, whereas single-reference teacher forcing rewards only one. Figure 3 (Top-left) shows that normalized text-token loss decreases much faster than speech-token loss, suggesting that textual response patterns are easier to learn from a single reference than fine-grained emotional expression. Accordingly, IF and EA improve steadily, while ES plateaus early and slightly declines. Single-reference supervision therefore learns what to say effectively, but is less suited to refining how to say it . GRPO. GRPO removes the single-reference constraint and enables reward-driven optimization, making it better suited to expressive generation. However, standard GRPO combines textual response quality and emotional expression into a single sequence-level reward: R = λ txt ​ R txt + λ sp ​ R sp . R=\\lambda^{\\mathrm{txt}}R^{\\mathrm{txt}}+\\lambda^{\\mathrm{sp}}R^{\\mathrm{sp}}. (3) A format reward with a fixed coefficient of 1 is also included in all runs but omitted here, since the analysis focuses on the trade-off between textual and acoustic rewards. The resulting shared advantage A A is applied uniformly to all token positions, without distinguishing which reward should guide text or speech tokens. Its induced credit structure can be intuitively decomposed as g ≈ g txt → txt + g sp → sp + g txt → sp + g sp → txt ⏟ cross-type credit assignment , g\\approx g^{\\text{txt}\\to\\text{txt}}+g^{\\text{sp}\\to\\text{sp}}+\\underbrace{g^{\\text{txt}\\to\\text{sp}}+g^{\\text{sp}\\to\\text{txt}}}_{\\text{cross-type credit assignment}}, (4) where g A → B g^{A\\to B} denotes the contribution from reward type A A to token type B B . This is an intuitive characterization, not a strict analytical decomposition, of how a shared advantage induces cross-type credit assignment. Figure 3 (Top-right) shows that increasing λ sp \\lambda^{\\mathrm{sp}} improves Emotion Performance but reduces Text Performance, with the reverse trend for λ txt \\lambda^{\\mathrm{txt}} ; reward weighting alone does not resolve this trade-off. In Figure 4 (a), the gradient norm ratio ∥ g sp ∥ ∥ g txt ∥ \\frac{\\lVert g^{\\mathrm{sp}}\\rVert}{\\lVert g^{\\mathrm{txt}}\\rVert} remains below 1.0 throughout GRPO training, indicating weaker speech-side magnitudes. In summary, SFT limits expressive diversity through single-reference supervision, while standard GRPO introduces reward-driven optimization but retains mismatched credit assignment across text and speech tokens. This motivates ALPO, which routes independent textual and acoustic advantages to their corresponding token subsets. ALPO To address the credit assignment mismatch in standard GRPO, we propose A coustic- L exical Decoupled P olicy O ptimization (ALPO). As illustrated in Figure 3 (Bottom), ALPO replaces the shared sequence-level advantage with separate advantages for different token types. Token partition. The generated response contains three token types: format tokens , which maintain the output structure (e.g., <tts_start> ); text tokens , which encode the response content; and speech tokens , which represent the acoustic realization. These types occupy non-overlapping vocabulary ranges and are each supervised by a reward for their primary function. Although speech generation is autoregressively conditioned on the preceding text, textual and acoustic rewards evaluate complementary aspects of the response. Reward design. ALPO uses three rewards: • Format Reward R fmt R^{\\mathrm{fmt}} is 1 if the output format is correct, and 0 otherwise. • Textual Reward R txt R^{\\mathrm{txt}} uses GLM-5 to assess the generated text for content quality, tone appropriateness, and conversational naturalness, normalized to [ 0 , 1 ] [0,1] . • Acoustic Reward R sp R^{\\mathrm{sp}} combines the emotion similarity of the generated speech to the paired reference and the target-emotion prototype, yielding a score in [ 0 , 1 ] [0,1] . The GLM-5 textual reward targets the same quality dimensions as RQ, while the acoustic reward directly corresponds to ES. GRPO and ALPO use identical rewards, isolating the policy optimization strategy. Model Intent Fulfillment Response Quality Emotion Accuracy Emotion Similarity Syn. Human Syn. Human Syn. Human Syn. Human Open-source Models GLM-4-Voice (Zeng et al. 2024 ) 33.44 30.96 45.18 41.55 40.24 35.78 43.45 42.88 MiMo-Audio (Zhang et al. 2025 ) 47.95 41.76 52.91 50.67 48.39 43.33 43.64 42.91 Qwen2.5-Omni (Xu et al. 2025a ) 32.78 28.64 35.68 35.12 45.94 39.89 42.21 41.27 Qwen3-Omni (Xu et al. 2025b ) 52.27 52.00 58.65 53.81 51.18 34.11 44.22 42.38 Step-Audio-2-mini Fine-tuned Variants Base (Wu et al. 2025 ) 41.48 37.17 45.87 40.04 57.88 45.87 42.23 41.88 SFT 91.74 79.11 84.02 73.81 88.14 66.22 42.99 42.39 DPO 91.28 82.39 83.49 72.37 89.15 71.33 45.29 44.66 GRPO † 91.99 79.67 84.60 74.43 87.35 69.33 49.37 48.73 Hybrid SFT-GRPO ‡ 87.60 77.44 80.40 72.85 72.86 62.89 45.35 44.27 ALPO (Ours) 92.28 81.61 85.51 77.84 88.72 71.78 51.92 51.25 Table 1: Automatic results on the synthetic and human-recorded ParaIntent test sets. Bold = best, underlined = second-best fine-tuned variant. GRPO † follows ParaS2S (Yang et al. 2025 ) ; Hybrid SFT-GRPO ‡ follows WavAlign (Chen et al. 2026 ) . Token-Specific Advantage Computation and Routing. ALPO independently group-normalizes each reward and applies its advantage only to the corresponding tokens. For k ∈ { fmt , txt , sp } k\\in\\{\\mathrm{fmt},\\mathrm{txt},\\mathrm{sp}\\} : A g k = R g k − μ k σ k + ε , A_{g}^{k}=\\frac{R_{g}^{k}-\\mu_{k}}{\\sigma_{k}+\\varepsilon}, (5) where μ k \\mu_{k} and σ k \\sigma_{k} are the group-wise mean and standard deviation for reward type k k . Let I k ​ ( y g ) I_{k}(y_{g}) index tokens of type k k in y g y_{g} . The per-type loss is: ℒ k ​ ( θ ) = \\displaystyle\\mathcal{L}^{k}(\\theta)= − 𝔼 1 G ∑ g = 1 G 1