{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9118f4adb5c912ab", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# WavAlign: Enhancing Intelligence and Expressiveness in Spoken Dialogue Models via Adaptive Hybrid Post-Training\narXiv: 2604.14932\nAuthors: Yifu Chen, Shengpeng Ji, Qian Chen, Tianle Liang, Yangzhuo Li, Ziqing Wang, Wen Wang, Jingyu Lu, Haoxiao Wang, Xueyi Pu, Fan Zhuo, Zhou Zhao\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: dialogue_system\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We identify and characterize key failure modes of unified sequence-level preference optimization for mixed text--speech outputs, including weak cross-modal coupling, gradient-energy imbalance, and noisy acoustic rewards.\n  2. We propose a single-stage adaptive hybrid post-training scheme that applies preference optimization to text tokens while anchoring speech tokens with SFT, coupled with a rollout-reliability gating mechanism for stable updates.\n  3. Experiments across architectures and benchmarks show consistent gains in both semantic quality and acoustic expressiveness.\n\nTags: LALM, LALM/对话型, LALM/后训练-DPO\n===\n# ParaS2S: Benchmarking and Aligning Spoken Language Models for Paralinguistic-aware Speech-to-Speech Interaction\narXiv: 2511.08723\nAuthors: Shu-wen Yang, Ming Tu, Andy T. Liu, Xinghua Qu, Hung-yi Lee, Lu Lu, Yuxuan Wang, Yonghui Wu\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: audio_understanding\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduction of ParaS2SBench, a new benchmark for evaluating paralinguistic-aware speech-to-speech dialogue that assesses both content and speaking style at the waveform level.\n  2. Development of a pipeline-based automatic evaluator for speech-to-speech interaction that mitigates style hallucination by separately modeling content and speaking style, outperforming end-to-end audio LLM judging.\n  3. Demonstration that the proposed automatic judge can successfully guide reinforcement learning post-training (ParaS2SAlign), achieving state-of-the-art performance on ParaS2SBench.\n  4. Evidence that reinforcement learning is more data-efficient than supervised fine-tuning for paralinguistic-aware speech-to-speech models, substantially reducing the need for expensive paired demonstrations.\n  5. Commitment to open-source the data, code, and models to facilitate future research in paralinguistic-aware speech interaction.\n\nTags: LALM, LALM/对话型, LALM/后训练-GRPO, Eval, Eval/听觉感知\n===\nPaper 2605.25604 not found\n===\n# GDPO: Group reward-Decoupled Normalization Policy Optimization for Multi-reward RL Optimization\narXiv: 2601.05242\nAuthors: Shih-Yang Liu, Xin Dong, Ximing Lu, Shizhe Diao, Peter Belcak, Mingjie Liu, Min-Hung Chen, Hongxu Yin, Yu-Chiang Frank Wang, Kwang-Ting Cheng, Yejin Choi, Jan Kautz, Pavlo Molchanov\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: speech_other\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We demonstrate that directly applying GRPO to normalize distinct rollout reward combinations causes them to collapse into identical advantage values, reducing the resolution of the training signal.\n  2. We propose GDPO, a new policy optimization method that resolves the GRPO collapse issue by decoupling the normalization of individual rewards to better preserve their relative differences.\n  3. We provide a systematic overview of how to modify reward functions and adjust reward weights to more faithfully align with preferences of varying priority.\n  4. We extensively evaluate GDPO across tool calling, math reasoning, and coding reasoning tasks, showing it consistently outperforms GRPO in convergence and downstream performance across a wide range of rewards.\n\nTags: LALM/后训练-GRPO", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "d96a0e59-f473-421d-becb-897d79b2b1c2", "uuid": "8ab62cc7-daa0-49f1-87de-25d080525ba9", "timestamp": "2026-08-05T08:04:15.664Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# WavAlign: Enhancing Intelligence and Expressiveness in Spoken Dialogue Models via Adaptive Hybrid Post-Training\narXiv: 2604.14932\nAuthors: Yifu Chen, Shengpeng Ji, Qian Chen, Tianle Liang, Yangzhuo Li, Ziqing Wang, Wen Wang, Jingyu Lu, Haoxiao Wang, Xueyi Pu, Fan Zhuo, Zhou Zhao\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: dialogue_system\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We identify and characterize key failure modes of unified sequence-level preference optimization for mixed text--speech outputs, including weak cross-modal coupling, gradient-energy imbalance, and noisy acoustic rewards.\n  2. We propose a single-stage adaptive hybrid post-training scheme that applies preference optimization to text tokens while anchoring speech tokens with SFT, coupled with a rollout-reliability gating mechanism for stable updates.\n  3. Experiments across architectures and benchmarks show consistent gains in both semantic quality and acoustic expressiveness.\n\nTags: LALM, LALM/对话型, LALM/后训练-DPO\n===\n# ParaS2S: Benchmarking and Aligning Spoken Language Models for Paralinguistic-aware Speech-to-Speech Interaction\narXiv: 2511.08723\nAuthors: Shu-wen Yang, Ming Tu, Andy T. Liu, Xinghua Qu, Hung-yi Lee, Lu Lu, Yuxuan Wang, Yonghui Wu\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: audio_understanding\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduction of ParaS2SBench, a new benchmark for evaluating paralinguistic-aware speech-to-speech dialogue that assesses both content and speaking style at the waveform level.\n  2. Development of a pipeline-based automatic evaluator for speech-to-speech interaction that mitigates style hallucination by separately modeling content and speaking style, outperforming end-to-end audio LLM judging.\n  3. Demonstration that the proposed automatic judge can successfully guide reinforcement learning post-training (ParaS2SAlign), achieving state-of-the-art performance on ParaS2SBench.\n  4. Evidence that reinforcement learning is more data-efficient than supervised fine-tuning for paralinguistic-aware speech-to-speech models, substantially reducing the need for expensive paired demonstrations.\n  5. Commitment to open-source the data, code, and models to facilitate future research in paralinguistic-aware speech interaction.\n\nTags: LALM, LALM/对话型, LALM/后训练-GRPO, Eval, Eval/听觉感知\n===\nPaper 2605.25604 not found\n===\n# GDPO: Group reward-Decoupled Normalization Policy Optimization for Multi-reward RL Optimization\narXiv: 2601.05242\nAuthors: Shih-Yang Liu, Xin Dong, Ximing Lu, Shizhe Diao, Peter Belcak, Mingjie Liu, Min-Hung Chen, Hongxu Yin, Yu-Chiang Frank Wang, Kwang-Ting Cheng, Yejin Choi, Jan Kautz, Pavlo Molchanov\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: speech_other\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We demonstrate that directly applying GRPO to normalize distinct rollout reward combinations causes them to collapse into identical advantage values, reducing the resolution of the training signal.\n  2. We propose GDPO, a new policy optimization method that resolves the GRPO collapse issue by decoupling the normalization of individual rewards to better preserve their relative differences.\n  3. We provide a systematic overview of how to modify reward functions and adjust reward weights to more faithfully align with preferences of varying priority.\n  4. We extensively evaluate GDPO across tool calling, math reasoning, and coding reasoning tasks, showing it consistently outperforms GRPO in convergence and downstream performance across a wide range of rewards.\n\nTags: LALM/后训练-GRPO", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
